 ## The swap's preparation, and the one way it could go stale (2026-10-23)
 
-While a turn waits, the warm ticks prepare most of the swap (`WorldEpoch._prep`: the carry weights, the new ring's slice hash, the kept and fresh places, the fresh places' layouts, the blended and platformed ground, the biome table), so the cut tick costs about a sixth of what it did (Performance's `docs/perf/arena-turn-2026-10-23.md`). The swap takes what is ready and makes the rest itself with the same functions (`E.unprep` counts it), so the turn is the same either way. **The risk: the preparation assumes that, between the decision and the cut, the base ground (`S.base`), the set of buildings (their places, columns and the list's members), `S.planetCols` and the epoch's places and districts do not change.** Today nothing changes them (`S.base` is written only at generation and by the swap; buildings are only marked, never added or removed, in a fight). **Anything that adds or removes a building or edits `S.base` while a turn is decided would make the preparation stale:** it would have to call `WorldEpoch.cancel`, or drop `WorldEpoch._plans`, so the swap recomputes. `epoch_check` has the equal-either-way test (the same state hash with the preparation and with it lost).
+While a turn waits, the warm ticks prepare most of the swap (`WorldEpoch._prep`: the carry weights, the new ring's slice hash, the kept and fresh places, the fresh places' layouts, the blended and platformed ground, the biome table), so the cut tick costs about a sixth of what it did (Performance's `docs/perf/arena-turn-2026-10-23.md`). The swap takes what is ready and makes the rest itself with the same functions (`WorldEpoch.unprepCount`, a process counter like `coldCount`, counts it), so the turn is the same either way. **The risk: the preparation assumes that, between the decision and the cut, the base ground (`S.base`), the set of buildings (their places, columns and the list's members), `S.planetCols` and the epoch's places and districts do not change.** Today nothing changes them (`S.base` is written only at generation and by the swap; buildings are only marked, never added or removed, in a fight). **Anything that adds or removes a building or edits `S.base` while a turn is decided would make the preparation stale:** it would have to call `WorldEpoch.cancel`, or drop `WorldEpoch._plans`, so the swap recomputes. `epoch_check` has the equal-either-way test (the same state hash with the preparation and with it lost).
+
+**Kept after the swap (2026-10-30).** The swap does not drop the ring job and the preparation it used: it retires them (`WorldEpoch._retire`), keyed by the world it turned away from, for `KEEP_TICKS` (21, NetSession's `maxAhead` 20 and one) sim ticks; `tick()` sweeps what has aged out, and `SimCore.dispose` releases a disposed sim's (`WorldEpoch.release`, which also drops every world the sim made entries for; `NetSnapshot.restore` disposes with `keepWorld`, since the restored state plays on). A rollback that restores a state of that world and runs the swap again finds them: the swap tick is the swap and not a ring read plus a preparation (`rerun_cost.gd`: about 60 ms before (90 to 107 on a loaded machine) and about 2 ms after, on a Half ring, with the same state hash). The kept preparation is safe to use twice because the swap binds its structures `fresh` (the prepared buildings and trees stay pristine) and the kept buildings' districts are generation records the restore puts back (packed-structures.md).
 
